# Core APP Generic Installation Wizard

Task: `AIPIFY.CORE.APP.GENERIC.INSTALLATION.WIZARD.V1`

## Ownership

- **Core** owns the authoritative `installation_contract` on `app_portal_integration_providers`.
- **Customer APP** only renders the generic `InstallationWizard` from the canonical Core contract.
- Platform Admin configures the contract; APP never invents provider-specific wizards.

## Hard rules

1. No provider-specific wizard components.
2. No customer-specific wizard components (including UnoNight special-cases).
3. No parallel provider registry — extend the existing provider row.
4. Minimize customer technical effort; assistance-first UX is mandatory.
5. Prefer Aipify-managed / guided over self-service when supported.
6. Dynamic global locale list from Core language foundation (`LOCALES`) — never hardcode locale tabs/unions in the wizard.
7. English fallback required; never show raw translation keys to customers.
8. Role-based presentation from the same contract (customer vs internal).
9. No raw technical/internal copy to customer audiences.
10. No activation before verified; no auto-activation from wizard completion alone.
11. No secret plaintext to client, logs, or session `field_values`.
12. Pause/resume required; session state is server-authoritative.
13. State transitions validated in Core RPCs — client cannot invent authority.
14. Reference fixtures are not live Production providers.
15. Premium, responsive, dark-mode, and accessibility requirements apply.
16. Authenticated Production proof is required before `PRODUCTION_READY`.
17. Connected integrations support safe read-only wizard preview via the same generic `InstallationWizard` renderer (`mode: "preview"`).
18. Preview must never create or mutate installation sessions, call test/verify/activate/save/invite, or read secret plaintext.
19. Support mode switching in preview is local UI state only and resets on close.
20. Preview is tenant-bound and requires integration manage authorization (`can_manage`).
21. Dynamic locale remains required for preview copy — no hardcoded locale lists.
22. Preview status and responsibility are derived from the locally selected support mode — never live waiting states (`awaiting_*`) before an external process starts.
23. Customer APP support choices are at most: Aipify-managed, guided, self-service, and IT (IT only when the contract allows). `partner_managed` is Core/Platform routing — never a direct customer choice.
24. Preview actions panel shows disabled examples only — no dead clickable controls, no Continue later, no invite/order/scheduling/test/activate.
25. Connect route (`/app/platform/integrations/connect/[providerKey]`) is explicit install mode via `AppPortalIntegrationSetupPanel` (`mode="install"`, `entry="connect_route"`).
26. Install support selection is local until explicit Fortsett — no session write on option select.
27. Install waiting states (`awaiting_*`) start only after a real invite/handoff request — never from selection alone.
28. Install status and responsibility share the same selectedSupportMode / lifecycle source.
29. Install action panel shows only relevant operational actions for the selected mode/lifecycle.
30. Continue later requires a real resumable installation session.
31. Live wrapper (`AppPortalIntegrationSetupPanel`) must be covered by integration/source tests — preview-only tests are insufficient for install runtime.
32. Handoff CTAs must map to a real tenant-bound server action (`POST …/installation/handoff`) — never a no-op or placeholder success.
33. Waiting states (`awaiting_*`) must be set only after a persisted handoff request succeeds — never before the request, and never from selection alone.
34. Never show an active customer CTA for `coming_later` / `invite_placeholder` actions; hide or honestly disable unimplemented modes.
35. Aipify-managed handoff creates a canonical `app_portal_installation_handoffs` request (plus audit and operational visibility) — not a client-only status flip.
36. Guided mode creates a guided setup request — never fake calendar booking.
37. Self-service confirms support mode and advances technical steps — it does not create an external waiting state.
38. Customer IT awaiting state requires a successful invitation handoff with a validated recipient — never placeholder invite + waiting.
39. All handoffs are tenant-bound, idempotent, and audited (session audit + handoff row).
40. Live CTA integration tests covering handoff wiring are mandatory for install runtime changes.
41. Customer-visible waiting labels must name the known responsible party; generic “responsible party” is fallback only — status copy must not alter lifecycle.
42. Customer-visible i18n must never render a raw key (`customerApp.*`). Waiting-party keys must be present in the Production `labels.setup.messageCatalog` allowlist (`buildInstallationWizardMessageCatalog`), not only in source locale JSON.
43. Runtime/i18n tests for waiting copy must use the Production-equivalent path: page builds `messageCatalog` via `buildAppPortalIntegrationsLabels`, client resolves through catalog lookup — source locale presence alone is insufficient.
44. Missing waiting-party copy falls back English → generic waiting (`waiting` / `waitingByParty.unknown`) → English customer-safe last resort; never raw key. Lifecycle, handoff, session, and APIs must not change for copy fixes.
45. Persisted open handoff has highest read-side priority over session ready_for_handoff / local selection — use `resolveHydratedInstallationState`.
46. Hydration must never create or mutate lifecycle (no POST/session write from GET hydrate). Matching open handoff hides handoff CTAs; refresh must preserve authoritative waiting presentation.
47. No wrong-state flash before handoff GET completes (`statusPendingHydration` / suppress CTAs). Read-side hydrate resolver must be covered by integration tests.

